# Non-Functional Requirements

Non-functional requirements define characteristics related to the system's quality, security, performance, and maintainability.

### NFR01 — Security

The system must protect sensitive information and use appropriate authentication and access control mechanisms.

### NFR02 — Performance

The system should provide adequate response times during operations performed by users.

### NFR03 — Availability

The system should remain available according to the infrastructure and environment in which it is deployed.

### NFR04 — Scalability

The architecture should allow the system to evolve and support new features without requiring major changes to its existing structure.

### NFR05 — Maintainability

The code should be organized and structured to facilitate maintenance and future development.

### NFR06 — Portability

The system should be able to run in different environments compatible with its technologies and dependencies.

### NFR07 — Containerization

The system should use Docker to simplify the setup, installation, and reproduction of development and production environments.

### NFR08 — Database

The system should use a relational database compatible with the application and support data storage, querying, and management operations.

### NFR09 — Responsiveness

The interface should adapt to different screen sizes and devices.

### NFR10 — Documentation

The project should provide sufficient documentation to allow users and developers to install, configure, use, and customize the system.

### NFR11 — Open Source

The project should allow its source code to be studied, modified, and distributed according to the terms defined by the project's license.
